item.description:
Computers are good with numbers.
&ldquo;what&rsquo;s bigger, centillition to the power of a centillion, or a googolplex?&rdquo;
I am glad that Google gives me a richly sourced and well reasoned answer, linked below:
&ldquo;A googolplex is vastly bigger than a centillion to the power of a centillion.&rdquo;
The Math
Centillion: In the short scale, a centillion is 10^303 (a 1 followed by 303 zeros) Centillion to the power of a centillion: (10^303)^(10^303) which is roughly 10^(10^305.

I had made an appointment with the car repair place, but I needed to cancel it. So I called there, and an AI answered. Transcript from memory.
AI: &ldquo;How can I help you?&rdquo;
Me: &ldquo;I need to cancel my appointment on Thursday.&rdquo;
AI: &ldquo;Sure, what is your name and the exact date of your appointment?&rdquo;
Me: &ldquo;Vrandečić. That&rsquo;s V-R-A—&rdquo;
AI: &ldquo;Thank you. Your appointment has been cancelled. Can I help you otherwise?

&ldquo;A loaf of bread please&rdquo;
&ldquo;I&rsquo;m sorry, I can&rsquo;t cut it because it&rsquo;s still hot.&rdquo;
&ldquo;GIVE. IT. TO. ME.&rdquo;

RIP Niklaus Wirth;
BEGIN
I don&rsquo;t think there&rsquo;s a person who created more programming languages that I used than Wirth: Pascal, Modula, and Oberon; maybe Guy Steele, depending on what you count;
Wirth is also famous for Wirth&rsquo;s law: software becomes slower more rapidly than hardware becomes faster;
He received the 1984 Turing Award, and had an asteroid named after him in 1999; Wirth died at the age of 89;

Now that the Croatian currency has died, they all come to the Gates of Heaven.
First goes the five kuna bill, and Saint Peter says &ldquo;Come in, you&rsquo;re welcome!&rdquo;
Then the ten kuna bill. &ldquo;Come in, you&rsquo;re welcome!&rdquo;
So does the twenty and fifty kuna bills. &ldquo;Come in, you&rsquo;re welcome!&rdquo;
Then comes the hundred kuna bill, expecting to walk in. Saint Peter looks up. &ldquo;Where do you think you&rsquo;re going?&rdquo;
